*   **Post Text:** What actually happens when a business gets Data Protection wrong? "It's just a privacy policy on a website", Until the Office of the Data Protection Commissioner actually calls. A lot of Kenyan businesses treat data protection enforcement as theoretical. It stopped being theoretical a while ago. Some real numbers: Since the Act took effect, the ODPC has logged over 9,000 complaints, resolved dozens through alternative dispute resolution, and issued hundreds of determinations, enforcement notices, penalty notices, and compensation orders. And 2025 alone saw the ODPC determine 96 complaints, nearly double the 2024 figure, marking a real shift from awareness-building to structured enforcement. A few cases that show what this looks like in practice: → Oppo Kenya: Hit with the ODPC's first-ever penalty notice, a KES 5 million fine, after posting a customer's photo on social media without consent, and then failing to cooperate with the enforcement process. → Platinum Credit: Ordered to pay a customer KES 900,000 after ignoring his requests to stop unwanted texts and delete his data. → Regus Kenya: Originally fined KES 5 million, later reduced to KES 2.5 million on appeal, with the High Court still upholding the ODPC's authority to enforce, a reminder that appeals reduce penalties, they don't make them disappear. Beyond the one-off fines, the maximum administrative penalty is KES 5 million or 1% of annual turnover, whichever is lower, and breaches must be reported to the ODPC within 72 hours of discovery. Ongoing violations can also attract daily fines of up to KES 10,000 for every day they go unresolved. The pattern across nearly every case, it wasn't the original mistake that turned expensive. It was not having a documented process to respond to a complaint, a request, or a breach when it landed. That's not a legal problem you fix with better wording. It's an operational gap, and it's exactly what I'm building to close. Has your business ever actually received a complaint, access request, or breach scare? And did you have a process ready, or were you improvising? #DataProtection #Compliance #Kenya #DPA2019 #ODPC #RiskConsulting… more
